### GraphQL Anti Patterns

#### 1. Exposing Entire Database Schema

```graphql
# Không nên - expose internal database structure
type User {
  id: ID!
  passwordHash: String!  # Security risk!
  internalNotes: String
  _internalId: Int!
  dbLastModified: DateTime
}
```

#### 2. N+1 Query Problem

```typescript
// Không nên - querying in loop
const resolvers = {
    Query: {
        users: () => db.getAllUsers(),
    },
    User: {
        posts: async (user) => {
            // N+1 query - bad!
            return db.query(`SELECT * FROM posts WHERE author_id = ${user.id}`);
        }
    }
};

// Nên - use DataLoader
const resolvers = {
    User: {
        posts: (user, args, { loaders }) => {
            return loaders.postsByUserLoader.load(user.id);
        }
    }
};
```

#### 3. Over-fetching without Pagination

```graphql
# Không nên - return all posts without pagination
type User {
    posts: [Post!]!  # Could be thousands!
}

# Nên - use connection pattern
type User {
    posts(
        first: Int
        after: String
    ): PostConnection!
}
```

#### 4. Missing Error Handling

```typescript
// Không nên
const resolvers = {
    Query: {
        user: async (_, { id }) => {
            return await db.users.findById(id);
        }
    }
};

// Nên
const resolvers = {
    Query: {
        user: async (_, { id }) => {
            const user = await db.users.findById(id);
            if (!user) {
                throw new NotFoundError('User', id);
            }
            return user;
        }
    }
};
```

#### 5. Mutations returning only boolean

```graphql
# Không nên - insufficient response
type Mutation {
    deleteUser(id: ID!): Boolean!
}

# Nên - return deleted ID
type Mutation {
    deleteUser(id: ID!): DeletePayload!
}

type DeletePayload {
    deletedId: ID
    success: Boolean!
    errors: [Error!]
}
```

### GraphQL Best Practices

#### 1. Schema Design Best Practices

```graphql
# Use meaningful naming conventions
type User {
    id: ID!
    firstName: String!      # Not: fn
    lastName: String!       # Not: ln
    emailAddress: String!   # Not: email
    createdAt: DateTime!    # Not: created_at
}

# Use meaningful input names
input CreateUserInput {
    firstName: String!
    lastName: String!
    email: String!
    password: String!
}

# Document everything
"This represents a user in the system"
type User {
    "Unique identifier"
    id: ID!
    
    "User's email address (only visible to owner and admins)"
    email: String!
    
    "User's display name"
    displayName: String!
}

# Use enums for limited values
enum OrderStatus {
    PENDING
    PROCESSING
    SHIPPED
    DELIVERED
    CANCELLED
}

# Use interfaces for shared types
interface Node {
    id: ID!
}

interface Timestamped {
    createdAt: DateTime!
    updatedAt: DateTime!
}
```

#### 2. Naming Conventions

```graphql
# Operations
type Query {
    # Nouns for reads
    user(id: ID!): User
    users(filter: UserFilter): UserConnection!
    
    # Plural for lists
    posts: [Post!]!
    comments: [Comment!]!
}

type Mutation {
    # Verbs for actions
    createUser(input: CreateUserInput!): UserPayload!
    updateUser(id: ID!, input: UpdateUserInput!): UserPayload!
    deleteUser(id: ID!): DeletePayload!
    publishPost(id: ID!): PostPayload!
}

type Subscription {
    # Past tense for events
    postCreated: Post!
    userSignedUp: User!
    orderShipped: Order!
}
```

#### 3. Relay Connection Pattern

```graphql
type PostConnection {
    edges: [PostEdge!]!
    pageInfo: PageInfo!
    totalCount: Int!
}

type PostEdge {
    node: Post!
    cursor: String!
}

type PageInfo {
    hasNextPage: Boolean!
    hasPreviousPage: Boolean!
    startCursor: String
    endCursor: String
}
```

#### 4. Federation-ready Schema

```graphql
# User service - schema.graphql
type User @key(fields: "id") {
    id: ID!
    email: String!
    name: String!
    posts: [Post!]!
}

extend type Post @key(fields: "id") {
    id: ID! @external
    author: User!
}
```

#### 5. Context Management

```typescript
import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';
import { Redis } from 'ioredis';

export interface GraphQLContext {
    prisma: PrismaClient;
    redis: Redis;
    user: User | null;
    ip: string;
    requestId: string;
}

export async function createContext({ req, res }): Promise<GraphQLContext> {
    const user = await authenticateUser(req);
    
    return {
        prisma: prismaClient,
        redis: redisClient,
        user,
        ip: req.ip,
        requestId: req.headers['x-request-id'] || generateRequestId()
    };
}
```

### GraphQL Workflow

#### 1. Schema Design

1. Gather requirements từ stakeholders
2. Identify entities và relationships
3. Design type definitions
4. Define queries và mutations
5. Add input types và enums
6. Review và refine schema
7. Document schema

#### 2. Implementation

1. Generate types from schema
2. Implement resolvers
3. Add validation
4. Configure DataLoaders
5. Add error handling
6. Implement authorization
7. Write tests

#### 3. Testing

1. Unit tests cho resolvers
2. Integration tests
3. Schema validation tests
4. Performance tests
5. Security tests

#### 4. Deployment

1. Deploy schema registry
2. Configure monitoring
3. Set up rate limiting
4. Configure caching
5. Document breaking changes

